  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 250 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA suppressed ridge pattern will continue throughout the majority\nof the long-term forecast period. Monsoonal ridging throughout\nthe weekend will gradually subside, with the 500mb jet generally\npositioned from the Washington/Oregon cascades and stretching\nacross the northern Rockies into the high plains of Montana and\nnorthern Wyoming. South of this feature, gradually deepening\nmoisture will spread across the central and southern Rockies\nfrom the Four Corners eastward through the high plains. PWAT\nvalues at selected sites across southeast Wyoming and western\nNebraska all show a steady rise each day from Sunday through the\nend of the week. This will lead to increasing coverage of\nshowers and thunderstorms each day. By the Wednesday-Friday\ntimeframe PWAT normalized anomalies in ensemble guidance will\nrun around 2 to 2.5 standard deviations above the mean. This\nwill result in high confidence of afternoon thunderstorms, some\nof which may contain heavy drenching downpours. Despite better\nthunderstorm chances, severe weather remains generally unlikely\nin southeast Wyoming with mostly light westerly flow in the mid\nand upper-levels present. Farther east into the Nebraska\nPanhandle, some faster flow aloft will begin to impinge on the\narea from the north by Wednesday as the aforementioned ridge of\nhigh pressure continues to become more suppressed to the south.\nLow-end wind shear values on the order of 30-40 knots may assist\nwith multicell storm modes that could produce organized outflow\nwind events during the afternoon hours from Wednesday through\nFriday. High confidence exists in above-normal temperatures\ncontinuing through the entire long-term period. However,\nincreasing coverage of afternoon thunderstorms from mid-week\nonward will likely limit temperatures somewhat, generally\nrunning around 10 degrees above normal instead of the near-\nrecord daily heat we will experience this weekend. Overall,\ngenerally high confidence exists in the long-term period with\nexcellent agreement in ensemble guidance with regard to synoptic\nforecast features.",
